<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shina-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shina language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shina is a Dardic Indo-Aryan language spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and in neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Kohistan. It is the main language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> and of several valleys, including parts of Nagar, and has many dialects. Long an unwritten language, it has been written in a modified Arabic-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> script since the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shina-language.jpg" alt="Shina language" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThatDohDude · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shina_in_Nastaliq.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Shina, also known by its exonym Gilgiti, is an Indo-Aryan language of the Dardic branch of the Indo-European family. It is spoken primarily by the Shina people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> and Kohistan. A small community of speakers lives in the Gurez valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and the Dras valley of Ladakh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Related outliers include Brokskat in Ladakh, Kundal Shahi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azad-kashmir">Azad Kashmir</a>, Palula and Sawi in Chitral and Kunar, Ushojo in the Swat Valley and Kalkoti in Dir.</p><p>Ethnologue estimated 1,146,000 speakers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 2018, most of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a>. In Ladakh, the speakers in the far north of Kargil district numbered about 32,200 in the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and in the Kashmir Valley Shina people form the majority in Gurez. Under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and English, and the religious weight of Arabic and Persian, the language keeps adding loan words.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Shina words are often distinguished by three contrasting tones: level, rising and falling. The tone is heard only on long vowels. Where the stress falls on the first mora of a long vowel the tone is falling, and where it falls on the second mora the tone is rising. The word written the means the imperative Do! with a level tone, while Thée means Will you do? and Theé means after having done. There are five vowels, each short or long, and all but one can also be nasalised. In the dialects of Dras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the original initial and final consonant clusters have been preserved, while those spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> have lost them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Shina is one of the few Dardic languages with a written tradition, yet it was unwritten until a few decades ago and remains mostly spoken. Since the first attempts to represent its phonology accurately in the 1960s, several orthographies have been proposed. Debates have centred on how to write retroflex sounds absent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and whether to mark vowel length and tone. Only in the late 2010s was the orthography standardised, and primers and dictionaries endorsed by the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> have been published. The alphabet is a Perso-Arabic one, developed by writers including Professor Muhammad Amin Ziya, Shakeel Ahmad Shakeel and Razwal Kohistani. Diacritics mark the rising and falling tones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shina language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
